# ── benchmark-data is a RECORD, as a whole ────────────────────────────────
#
# Stated as ONE rule rather than accumulated file by file. Every ghcr tag under
# benchmark-data/ names the image that PRODUCED the numbers beside it, so
# advancing any of them attributes a measurement to a toolchain that never ran
# it. Two entries above already say exactly this, one file at a time
# (DYNAMIC_IR_REGENERATED.md, SOURCE_MANIFEST.md) — and the caravel landing then
# surfaced seven more in one cell (scan_chain.json, container_image.json x3,
# vibe_ic_one_shot.json x3), which is how a per-file list behaves: it is always
# one evidence cell behind, and the gap is silent until the next landing.
#
# `_matches` runs fnmatch against the full relative path, so the glob covers
# every cell, present and future. A published run's record is never rewritten to
# agree with today's anchor; if the anchor must change, a NEW run produces a NEW
# record.
benchmark-data/*

# vibe-ic#735 — the test that proves the run image reaches the child `docker run`.
# Its tags are FIXTURE DATA, not live pointers, and rewriting them to the current
# anchor would destroy what the test measures:
#   * the module docstring records an OBSERVED run (`image_ref: :0.2.58` verified,
#     the `:0.2.60` candidates absent, `hpretl/iic-osic-tools:latest` PRESENT and
#     what DFT actually used). Re-stamping a recorded measurement makes it a
#     fiction.
#   * the assertions need the verified tag and the used tag to DIFFER — that
#     difference IS the defect being pinned. Setting both to the anchor makes the
#     test pass on the broken code it was written to catch.
vibe-ic-marketplace/plugins/vibe-ic/programs/tests/test_run_image_propagates_to_child_docker_run.py
